WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Two nuclei, each dotted with bright spots of a glowing protein.

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.180
Is a spot a droplet of that protein?

3
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.540
Or is it a cluster of binding sites, such as many copies of one DNA sequence?

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.980
Under a microscope, the two can look alike.

5
00:00:20.517 --> 00:00:25.757
A nucleus has no membrane-bound compartments inside it, yet it holds many bodies

6
00:00:25.757 --> 00:00:31.017
like these. The largest, the nucleolus, builds ribosomal subunits.

7
00:00:32.437 --> 00:00:37.057
Many of their molecules carry several weak sticky patches, so each can hold many

8
00:00:37.057 --> 00:00:40.537
partners at once. That is multivalency.

9
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.507
The usual checks cannot tell droplets from sites.

10
00:00:45.787 --> 00:00:47.387
Both kinds of spot look round.

11
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:52.807
Droplets fuse, and site clusters can drift together until they look merged.

12
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:59.167
Bleach one with a laser, and it glows again, because molecules that bind and let go

13
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:04.747
refill it too. A bright spot shows only that the protein is concentrated there.

14
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:07.487
So change the amount instead.

15
00:01:08.827 --> 00:01:13.867
Give both nuclei one dial: the total protein, averaged over the whole nucleus.

16
00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:18.287
Add a gauge that reads the concentration outside the spots.

17
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.227
Now double the protein in both.

18
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:24.007
What will each outside gauge do?

19
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:31.713
The droplet needle holds still.

20
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:38.253
Think of salt water past saturation: added salt stops dissolving and forms a second

21
00:01:38.253 --> 00:01:44.753
phase. At equilibrium under fixed conditions, one protein on its own holds the

22
00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:46.893
outside at the saturation concentration.

23
00:01:48.193 --> 00:01:52.053
The extra builds more of a second, denser phase: the droplets.

24
00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:55.673
They take up more room, but glow no brighter.

25
00:01:57.173 --> 00:01:58.173
Bigger, not brighter.

26
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:01.277
On the site side, the needle climbs.

27
00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:07.257
Think of the sites as parking spaces, a fixed number, with plenty still free.

28
00:02:08.557 --> 00:02:13.617
Some protein is parked and the rest is outside, so the outside rises with the total.

29
00:02:14.877 --> 00:02:17.817
The spots fill in and glow brighter, but grow no bigger.

30
00:02:19.237 --> 00:02:20.237
Brighter, not bigger.

31
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:29.930
Keep turning. On the site side, the parking lot fills up.

32
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:36.570
The spots brighten more slowly, and nearly every extra molecule stays outside, so

33
00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.950
that needle climbs faster.

34
00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:43.570
Within this range, the droplets keep taking up more room, and their needle stays

35
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:44.570
put.

36
00:02:44.983 --> 00:02:47.783
So here is the test, in its simplest form.

37
00:02:49.163 --> 00:02:51.743
Add more protein, and watch where the extra goes.

38
00:02:53.063 --> 00:02:58.263
At equilibrium under fixed conditions, one protein forming a phase puts it into more

39
00:02:58.263 --> 00:03:00.863
droplet volume, outside held still.

40
00:03:02.163 --> 00:03:06.743
A fixed set of sites puts it onto those sites: brighter spots, until they are full.

41
00:03:07.817 --> 00:03:12.017
This clean version is for one protein that separates on its own.

42
00:03:13.257 --> 00:03:18.897
Real bodies hold many kinds of molecule, some may form on chromatin, and tiny spots

43
00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:20.277
blur size into brightness.

44
00:03:21.597 --> 00:03:24.157
So where the extra goes is a clue, not a proof.

45
00:03:25.417 --> 00:03:28.497
The text below shows what each complication changes.
